/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A DC motor has a stationary field (permanent magnets or a field winding) and a rotating armature whose coils are fed through brushes and a commutator.
   - E = kΦ ω, V = E + I Ra, T = kΦ I
   - E is back emf in V; kΦ is the machine constant in V·s/rad (equal to N·m/A); ω is speed in rad/s; V is the supply voltage; I is armature current in A; Ra is armature resistance in Ω; T is torque in N·m.
   - Put together, ω = (V − I Ra) ÷ kΦ: a permanent-magnet motor runs fastest with no load, and its speed falls in a straight line as torque (and current) rise, down to zero at the stall current V ÷ Ra.
   - A three-phase machine has three stator windings 120° apart.
   - ns = 120 f ÷ p, s = (ns − n) ÷ ns
   - ns is synchronous speed in rpm; f is the supply frequency in Hz; p is the number of poles; s is the slip of an induction machine; n is the rotor speed in rpm.
   - Single-phase AC machines (universal motors, capacitor-start and shaded-pole induction motors) run from mains AC.
   - A motor's limit is heat.
   - CDF = ton ÷ (ton + toff) × 100 %
*/
